Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics

A Control-Theoretic Framework for AI-Designed RNA Medicine, Virtual Cells, and Closed-Loop Therapeutic Intervention

Version 0.3

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI collaboration: ChatGPT, DeepSeek, Kimi, Meta AI

---

Abstract

Medicine is transitioning from molecule-centric discovery toward programmable therapeutic intervention. RNA therapeutics, programmable lipid nanoparticles, AI virtual cells, patient digital twins, and closed-loop experimental platforms make it possible to formulate therapy as a design and control problem rather than solely as a discovery problem. This manuscript develops Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics, a formal framework that treats therapeutic intervention as a constrained inverse-control problem over partially observed, uncertain biological dynamical systems. The central hypothesis is that future therapeutic systems can be represented as a closed loop:

\text{Sense} \rightarrow \text{Infer} \rightarrow \text{Design} \rightarrow \text{Verify} \rightarrow \text{Intervene} \rightarrow \text{Measure} \rightarrow \text{Learn}.

The framework distinguishes observational predictive models from interventional causal models, treats delivery as a coupled dynamical subsystem, and replaces naïve safety constraints with predictive safety filters, control barrier functions, formal verification, and conformal prediction. A therapeutic programmability assessment is proposed as a multi-criteria vector rather than a single index, and a programmability frontier is defined across four operational tiers. A worked example for siRNA-mediated PCSK9 control illustrates the framework without claiming empirical results. The contribution is a formal, falsifiable research program for therapeutic systems engineering.

---

1. Introduction

For much of modern pharmacology, therapeutic discovery has been exploratory. Researchers identify mechanisms, screen candidate molecules, test them experimentally, and optimize promising leads. This paradigm has produced major advances, but it remains constrained by the dimensionality and partial observability of biological systems.

A therapeutic intervention is not a molecule acting on an isolated target. Its effect depends on molecular interactions, cellular state, tissue context, delivery, metabolism, immune response, patient variation, and temporal dynamics. Programmable biological modalities change the engineering problem. RNA can be designed as a compact regulator of gene expression. Programmable lipid nanoparticles can control organ tropism, cell specificity, intracellular trafficking, and immune interaction. The therapeutic object is therefore becoming multidimensional:

u = (s, \mathcal{L}, \rho, \tau),

where s is the molecular sequence or therapeutic program, \mathcal{L} is the delivery configuration, \rho is the dose, and \tau is the temporal administration policy. Throughout the manuscript, the stage cost of a control problem will be denoted c(x,u) to avoid notational conflict with the dose variable.

The fundamental question becomes:

\text{Given a biological objective, what intervention should be designed?}

This is an inverse problem. Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics formalizes therapeutic design as a constrained inverse-control problem over a partially observed biological dynamical system. The resulting architecture is a closed loop, in which sensing, inference, design, verification, intervention, measurement, and learning operate recursively.

The central hypothesis is deliberately narrow:

A sufficiently accurate, uncertainty-aware model of a biological system, combined with programmable therapeutic actuators, continuous biological observation, and safety-constrained adaptive control, can transform selected areas of medicine from static intervention toward closed-loop therapeutic regulation.

The objective is to determine which biological systems are programmable, how programmability can be quantified, and what mathematical conditions are required to control them safely.

---

2. Related Work and Positioning

Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics intersects several active research areas but differs from each in a specific way.

AI drug discovery has focused on generative molecular design, protein structure prediction, and virtual screening. These methods typically solve static design problems: given a target, propose a molecule. Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics instead addresses dynamic intervention: given a desired biological trajectory, select an intervention policy under safety constraints.

Computational systems biology has developed mechanistic and constraint-based models of cellular networks. These models are often deterministic or stochastic simulators, but they are rarely embedded in a real-time control loop with formal safety guarantees. Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics uses such models as predictive engines inside a safety-constrained control architecture.

Closed-loop medical devices, such as artificial pancreas systems and deep brain stimulators, already implement sense-infer-actuate loops at timescales of minutes to milliseconds. Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics extends this principle to molecular and cellular therapeutics, where interventions occur at timescales of hours to days and the actuator is a programmable biological agent rather than an electrical or mechanical device.

Precision medicine and pharmacogenomics typically personalize static drug selection or dosing based on biomarkers. Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics treats the therapeutic policy itself as an adaptive object that changes with the evolving state of the patient.

A substantial body of work has applied model-predictive control to therapeutic optimization, including cancer chemotherapy scheduling, HIV combination therapy, anesthesia depth control, and glucose regulation. These applications typically use low-dimensional physiological models, such as pharmacokinetic compartments, and operate at timescales of minutes to hours. Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics differs in three respects. First, the state space is high-dimensional and partially observed, requiring latent-space methods. Second, the actuator is a programmable molecular agent with its own delivery dynamics. Third, the framework requires causal virtual-cell models and formal safety verification, not merely predictive optimization.

Thus, Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics is not simply systems biology plus model-predictive control plus RNA. It is a specific integration of causal virtual-cell modeling, programmable molecular actuators, formal safety verification, and patient-specific online inference, aimed at diseases where the relevant state is partially observable but sufficiently controllable.

