3. Biological Systems as Partially Observed Dynamical Systems

The complete biological state cannot be directly observed. Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics formulates the patient as a stochastic, partially observed dynamical system:

\begin{aligned}
x_{t+1} &= f(x_t, u_t, \theta) + w_t,\\
y_t &= h(x_t) + v_t,
\end{aligned}

where x_t \in \mathbb{R}^{n_x} is the latent biological state, u_t \in \mathcal{U} is the therapeutic intervention, \theta \in \mathbb{R}^{n_\theta} denotes patient-specific parameters, w_t is process noise, y_t \in \mathbb{R}^{n_y} denotes available observations, and v_t is measurement noise.

Observations may include circulating biomarkers, transcriptomic or proteomic measurements, imaging, and clinical signs. The therapeutic controller operates on an estimated state \hat{x}_t rather than the true state x_t. A safe programmable medicine system must therefore jointly solve state estimation, system identification, and therapeutic control.

3.1 Observability Bottleneck

For a linearized system around a nominal trajectory,

\Delta x_{t+1} = A \Delta x_t + B \Delta u_t, \qquad \Delta y_t = C \Delta x_t,

the finite-horizon observability Gramian is

W_o = \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} (A^\top)^k C^\top C A^k.

If \operatorname{rank}(W_o) \ll \dim(x), many state directions are unobservable. In most human diseases, observations are sparse, delayed, and destructive. Single-cell measurements require biopsy; circulating biomarkers only partially reflect intracellular state. Therefore, for many diseases, the system is severely partially observable.

Proposition 1 (Local observability).
If \operatorname{rank}(W_o) = n_x, then the linearized system is observable on the horizon H. If \operatorname{rank}(W_o) < n_x, there exist state directions that cannot be reconstructed from the available measurements on that horizon.

Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics treats observability as a first-class limitation. A disease is not programmable unless its relevant state variables can be measured, directly or indirectly, with sufficient accuracy and timeliness.

3.2 State Estimation

Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics adopts a Bayesian state estimator in which a learned dynamical model serves as the proposal distribution. A standard recursive formulation is

p(x_t \mid y_{0:t}) \propto p(y_t \mid x_t) \int p(x_t \mid x_{t-1}, u_{t-1}) p(x_{t-1} \mid y_{0:t-1})\, dx_{t-1}.

In practice, this can be implemented as a particle filter, an ensemble Kalman filter, or a latent-space Kalman filter:

\hat{x}_t = \mathcal{E}\big(y_t, \hat{x}_{t-1}, u_{t-1}, \mathcal{V}\big),

where \mathcal{V} is a virtual cell model. The virtual cell is used to propagate belief, not to replace measurement.

For linear Gaussian systems,

x_{t+1} = A x_t + B u_t + w_t,\quad w_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0,Q),

y_t = C x_t + v_t,\quad v_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0,R),

the posterior is exactly Gaussian, and the Kalman filter gives the optimal recursive update. This is the baseline formal engine for Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics state estimation.

