5. From Virtual Cell to Patient Digital Twin

The virtual cell is not the same as a patient digital twin:

\text{Virtual Cell} \neq \text{Patient Digital Twin}.

A virtual cell attempts to represent cellular dynamics across biological contexts. A therapeutic digital twin attempts to represent an individual patient’s relevant biological state and response characteristics.

We define

\mathcal{T}_i = (\hat{x}_i, \hat{\theta}_i, \mathcal{V}),

where i denotes patient i. The digital twin updates continuously:

\hat{\theta}_{t+1} = \hat{\theta}_t + \Delta\theta(y_t, \hat{x}_t),

where \Delta\theta is derived from the natural gradient of the evidence lower bound or from a Kalman gain update. This allows the system to progressively personalize therapeutic predictions.

For patient heterogeneity, we adopt a hierarchical Bayesian formulation. Population parameters are drawn from a prior:

\theta_i \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_{\text{pop}}, \Sigma_{\text{pop}}),

and patient-specific observations follow:

y_t \sim p(y_t \mid x_t, \theta_i).

Online inference maintains a posterior q(\theta_i \mid y_{0:t}) using variational inference or an ensemble Kalman filter.

Proposition 2 (Recursive ridge update).
Suppose \theta has prior \mathcal{N}(\mu_{\text{pop}}, \Sigma_{\text{pop}}), and observations follow

y_t = H_t \theta + v_t,\quad v_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0,R_t).

Then the posterior after t observations is \mathcal{N}(\hat\theta_t, \Sigma_t), where

\Sigma_t^{-1} = \Sigma_{\text{pop}}^{-1} + \sum_{k=0}^t H_k^\top R_k^{-1} H_k,

\hat\theta_t = \Sigma_t \left( \Sigma_{\text{pop}}^{-1}\mu_{\text{pop}} + \sum_{k=0}^t H_k^\top R_k^{-1} y_k \right).

The posterior concentrates around the true parameter if the observation matrices are persistently exciting. Without persistent excitation, patient-specific inference remains uncertain, and Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics falls back to population priors.

