8. Safety as a Hard Constraint

A major difference between conventional AI drug prediction and therapeutic control is the requirement for explicit safety guarantees.

Define the safe biological region:

\mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}} = \{ x : b(x) \ge 0 \},

where b(x) is a learned or biologically defined barrier function.

The admissible intervention set is:

\mathcal{U}_{\text{safe}} \subseteq \mathcal{U}.

For uncertain systems, we require:

P\left( x_t \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}} \ \forall t \right) \ge 1 - \epsilon,

where \epsilon is an acceptable residual risk bound.

8.1 Control Barrier Functions

Set membership alone is insufficient. Modern safe control uses control barrier functions to enforce forward invariance.

In discrete time, a standard condition is:

b(x_{t+1}) - b(x_t) \ge -\alpha b(x_t),

with 0 < \alpha \le 1. This ensures that if b(x_t) \ge 0, then b(x_{t+1}) \ge (1-\alpha)b(x_t), so the system remains safe.

Lemma 1 (Forward invariance).
If the discrete-time control barrier function condition holds for all t, and b(x_0) \ge 0, then b(x_t) \ge 0 for all t \ge 0.

Proof. The condition implies b(x_{t+1}) \ge (1-\alpha)b(x_t). Since 1-\alpha \ge 0, if b(x_t) \ge 0, then b(x_{t+1}) \ge 0. The result follows by induction. \square

8.2 Predictive Safety Filter

Before applying a proposed intervention u_t^*, a predictive safety filter checks whether the intervention preserves safety.

The filter solves:

u_t = \arg\min_{u \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{safe}}} \| u - u_t^* \|^2,

subject to

b(\hat{x}_{t+1 \mid t}) \ge (1-\alpha) b(\hat{x}_t),

and the existence of a backup trajectory that can steer the system to a terminal safe set.

If no feasible intervention exists, the filter applies a precomputed safe fallback policy u_{\text{safe}}.

8.3 Conformal Safety

Because learned models are imperfect, point predictions are insufficient. Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics uses conformal prediction to provide finite-sample uncertainty sets under exchangeability.

Given a learned model \hat{f}, a conformal predictor constructs a set C_t(x_t,u_t) such that, for exchangeable calibration and test points,

P\big(x_{t+1} \in C_t(x_t,u_t)\big) \ge 1 - \epsilon

under exchangeability assumptions.

The safety filter then requires:

\forall \tilde{x}_{t+1} \in C_t(x_t,u_t), \quad b(\tilde{x}_{t+1}) \ge (1-\alpha)b(x_t).

This provides a distribution-free, finite-sample safety certificate under exchangeability.

However, standard conformal guarantees assume exchangeability. Patient time series are temporally dependent and subject to distribution shift. Moreover, the closed-loop control policy selects actions based on all previous observations, inducing dependence across time steps. Therefore, the conformal filter is best interpreted as a per-step probabilistic shield rather than a formal finite-horizon trajectory safety certificate.

Theorem 1 (Conformal safety filter under exchangeability).
Assume that at each time step the calibration and test points are exchangeable, and that the conformal set C_t(x_t,u_t) has marginal coverage at least 1-\epsilon_t, where \epsilon_t = \epsilon/H. If the safety filter enforces

b(\tilde{x}_{t+1}) \ge (1-\alpha)b(x_t)

for all \tilde{x}_{t+1} \in C_t(x_t,u_t), then on each event x_{t+1}\in C_t(x_t,u_t), the true next state satisfies the barrier inequality. Because closed-loop adaptive interventions induce dependence across steps, a finite-horizon joint guarantee cannot be obtained by a union bound without further assumptions. Under independence of miscoverage events, the union bound gives

P\left(x_t\in\mathcal{S}\ \forall t\in[0,H]\right)\ge 1-\epsilon.

Proof sketch. At each step, with probability at least 1-\epsilon_t, the true next state lies in C_t(x_t,u_t). On that event, the filter implies b(x_{t+1}) \ge (1-\alpha)b(x_t). Since b(x_t)\ge0 implies b(x_{t+1})\ge0, induction maintains safety. If miscoverage events are independent, the union bound yields the joint guarantee. Without independence, the composition is not formally justified. \square

Adaptive conformal inference methods, such as Adaptive Conformal Inference and Sequential Predictive Conformal Inference, provide long-run empirical coverage under distribution shift but do not generally provide the finite-sample closed-loop guarantee needed for high-risk autonomous control. This limitation is recorded as an open problem in Section 12.

8.4 Formal Verification of Clinical Safety Constraints

Probabilistic constraints alone are insufficient for high-risk autonomous decisions. Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics incorporates formal verification for the decision layer. Safety constraints can be encoded as invariant constraints, temporal logic properties, and satisfiability-modulo-theories-verifiable rule sets for the decision layer, with neural prediction confined to the state-estimation layer. This separates concerns: learned models provide uncertain state estimates; formal verification ensures that the high-level therapeutic policy satisfies hard clinical constraints over the entire input space, not merely on a sampled test set.

8.5 Structural Uncertainty and Model Misspecification

The safety architecture assumes that the virtual cell captures the true biological dynamics up to bounded noise and Lipschitz error. Biological systems, however, exhibit compensatory pathways and emergent behaviors that violate this assumption. For example, PCSK9 knockdown can upregulate SREBP-2 and LDL receptor expression through feedback, which may be absent from a training dataset.

Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics distinguishes parametric uncertainty, handled by Bayesian adaptation, from structural uncertainty, arising from a misspecified model class. To mitigate structural uncertainty, an ensemble safety filter is introduced. Let \mathcal{V}_1,\dots,\mathcal{V}_K be K virtual cell models with diverse architectures. For each model j, compute the predicted next state set C_t^{(j)}(x_t,u_t). An intervention is considered structurally safe only if the safety condition holds for every ensemble member:

\forall j \in \{1,\dots,K\}, \quad \forall \tilde{x}_{t+1} \in C_t^{(j)}(x_t,u_t), \quad b(\tilde{x}_{t+1}) \ge (1-\alpha)b(x_t).

If the ensemble members disagree on the safety of a proposed action, the filter defaults to the most conservative feasible action or to the precomputed fallback policy. Structural uncertainty is the primary reason Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics restricts higher autonomy levels even when parametric uncertainty is well characterized.

