9. Therapeutic Model-Predictive Control

At each decision time t, the system solves:

\min_{u_{t:t+H-1}} \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} c(x_{t+k}, u_{t+k}) + V_f(x_{t+H}),

subject to

x_{t+k+1} = F(x_{t+k}, u_{t+k}, \hat{\theta}_t),

and

x_{t+k} \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}}, \qquad u_{t+k} \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{safe}},

where c(x,u) is a positive definite stage cost and V_f is a terminal cost. Only the first intervention is executed:

u_t = u_t^*.

The system then observes the biological response and recomputes. Thus,

\text{predict} \rightarrow \text{intervene} \rightarrow \text{observe} \rightarrow \text{recalculate}.

This is the core closed-loop principle of Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics.

9.1 Recursive Feasibility and Stability

Theorem 2 (Recursive feasibility of model-predictive control).
If the model-predictive control problem is feasible at time 0, then it is feasible at every subsequent time under the receding-horizon policy, provided that a terminal controller \kappa_f(x) exists such that for all x\in\mathcal{S}_f,

f(x,\kappa_f(x)) \in \mathcal{S}_f,

and

V_f(f(x,\kappa_f(x))) - V_f(x) \le -c(x,\kappa_f(x)).

Proof sketch. Let the optimal control sequence at time t be u_{t:t+H-1}^*, with associated state trajectory ending in x_{t+H}\in\mathcal{S}_f. At time t+1, a feasible candidate is u_{t+1:t+H-1}^*, u_{t+H}=\kappa_f(x_{t+H}). By the terminal invariance assumption, this candidate satisfies all constraints, so feasibility is preserved. \square

Theorem 3 (Stability of model-predictive control).
Under the assumptions of Theorem 2 and a positive definite stage cost, the origin is asymptotically stable for the closed-loop system.

Proof sketch. Define the value function

J^*(x) = \min_{u} \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} c(x_k,u_k) + V_f(x_H).

By the Bellman inequality and terminal cost condition,

J^*(x_{t+1}) - J^*(x_t) \le -c(x_t,u_t).

Since c is positive definite, J^* is a Lyapunov function, and asymptotic stability follows. \square

For tracking problems where x^* \neq 0, the stability result applies after transforming to deviation coordinates \tilde{x} = x - x^*. Offset-free tracking can be achieved by augmenting the state with an integrator or by using a parameterized terminal set.

9.2 Robust Model-Predictive Control

When process noise is bounded, w_t \in \mathcal{W}, constraint tightening is used:

x_{t+k} \in \mathcal{S} \ominus \mathcal{R}_k,

where \mathcal{R}_k is the reachable set of disturbances. Recursive feasibility and robust constraint satisfaction can be guaranteed using tube-based model-predictive control. This is essential because the virtual cell does not match the patient exactly.

9.3 Latent-Space Model-Predictive Control

Biological states are extremely high-dimensional: transcriptomic states can contain more than 20,000 genes, and proteomic states more than 10,000 proteins. Solving model-predictive control in the full state space is computationally intractable. Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics therefore solves model-predictive control in a learned low-dimensional latent space.

Let \phi_\theta: \mathbb{R}^{n_x} \to \mathbb{R}^{n_z} be an encoder and \psi_\theta: \mathbb{R}^{n_z} \to \mathbb{R}^{n_x} a decoder, with n_z \ll n_x. The latent dynamics are

z_{t+1} = F_\theta(z_t, u_t),

where F_\theta is learned by operator inference on proper orthogonal decomposition modes or by a neural ordinary differential equation. State estimation is performed in the latent space using an unscented Kalman filter or particle filter:

\hat{z}_t = \text{UKF}\big(\phi_\theta(y_t), \hat{z}_{t-1}, u_{t-1}\big).

The model-predictive control problem is then solved in the latent space:

\min_{u_{t:t+H-1}} \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} c_z(z_{t+k}, u_{t+k}) + V_{f,z}(z_{t+H}),

subject to latent dynamics and safety constraints mapped through the decoder. This reduces computational complexity from cubic or worse in n_x to cubic in n_z, where n_z may be on the order of 10–100.

