Appendix F. Model-Predictive Control: Recursive Feasibility and Stability

F.1 Problem formulation

At each time t, the model-predictive control problem is

\min_{u_{t:t+H-1}}
\sum_{k=0}^{H-1} c(x_{t+k},u_{t+k}) + V_f(x_{t+H})

subject to

x_{t+k+1}=f(x_{t+k},u_{t+k}),

x_{t+k}\in\mathcal{S},\quad u_{t+k}\in\mathcal{U},

x_{t+H}\in\mathcal{S}_f.

Here c(x,u) is a positive definite stage cost and V_f is a terminal cost.

F.2 Assumptions for stability

1. The stage cost c(x,u) is continuous, c(0,0)=0, and there exists \alpha_1\in\mathcal{K}_\infty such that c(x,u)\ge \alpha_1(\|x\|).
2. There exists a terminal controller \kappa_f(x) such that for all x\in\mathcal{S}_f,
   f(x,\kappa_f(x))\in\mathcal{S}_f,
   and
   V_f(f(x,\kappa_f(x)))-V_f(x)\le -c(x,\kappa_f(x)).
3. The terminal set \mathcal{S}_f is a subset of \mathcal{S}.

F.3 Theorem: recursive feasibility

If the model-predictive control problem is feasible at time 0, then it is feasible at every subsequent time under the receding-horizon policy.

Proof.
Let the optimal control sequence at time t be

u_{t:t+H-1}^*.

The associated state trajectory ends in x_{t+H}\in\mathcal{S}_f. At time t+1, a feasible candidate is

u_{t+1:t+H-1}^*,\quad u_{t+H}=\kappa_f(x_{t+H}).

By the terminal invariance assumption, this candidate satisfies all constraints, so feasibility is preserved. \square

F.4 Theorem: stability of model-predictive control

Under the assumptions of F.2 and recursive feasibility, the origin is asymptotically stable for the closed-loop system.

Proof.
Define the value function

J^*(x)=\min_{u} \sum_{k=0}^{H-1}c(x_k,u_k)+V_f(x_H).

By the Bellman inequality and terminal cost condition,

J^*(x_{t+1})-J^*(x_t)\le -c(x_t,u_t).

Since c is positive definite, J^* is a Lyapunov function, and asymptotic stability follows. \square

Remark on tracking.
For tracking problems where x^*\neq 0, transform to deviation coordinates \tilde x=x-x^* and restate the stability result for \tilde x\to 0. Offset-free tracking can be achieved by augmenting the state with an integrator or by using a parameterized terminal set.

F.5 Robust model-predictive control with bounded disturbances

When process noise is bounded, w_t\in\mathcal{W}, constraint tightening is used:

x_{t+k}\in\mathcal{S}\ominus \mathcal{R}_k,

where \mathcal{R}_k is the reachable set of disturbances. Recursive feasibility and robust constraint satisfaction can be guaranteed using tube-based model-predictive control.

F.6 Latent-space model-predictive control

Let \phi_\theta:\mathbb{R}^{n_x}\to\mathbb{R}^{n_z} be an encoder and \psi_\theta:\mathbb{R}^{n_z}\to\mathbb{R}^{n_x} a decoder, with n_z\ll n_x. The latent dynamics are

z_{t+1}=F_\theta(z_t,u_t).

State estimation is performed in the latent space using an unscented Kalman filter or particle filter:

\hat z_t=\text{UKF}\big(\phi_\theta(y_t),\hat z_{t-1},u_{t-1}\big).

The model-predictive control problem is solved in the latent space:

\min_{u_{t:t+H-1}}
\sum_{k=0}^{H-1} c_z(z_{t+k},u_{t+k}) + V_{f,z}(z_{t+H}),

subject to latent dynamics and safety constraints mapped through the decoder. This reduces computational complexity from cubic or worse in n_x to cubic in n_z.

