Appendix G. Delivery Co-Design as Bilevel Optimization

G.1 Formulation

The delivery co-design problem is

\min_{\mathcal{L}\in\mathcal{L}_{\text{delivery}}} \mathcal{J}_{\text{outer}}(\mathcal{L})

subject to

\mathcal{J}_{\text{outer}}(\mathcal{L})=
\min_{s,\rho,\tau} D\big(F(x,z),x^*\big)+\lambda \mathcal{C}_{\text{cost}}(u),

z_{t+1}=g(z_t,\mathcal{L}_t,\eta_t),

x_{t+1}=f(x_t,s_t\cdot h(z_t),\theta,w_t),

and safety constraints. Here \mathcal{C}_{\text{cost}}(u) denotes the intervention cost, distinct from the conformal set C_t.

G.2 Existence of optimal solution

If \mathcal{L}_{\text{delivery}} is compact, g and f are continuous, and the inner problem has a continuous value function, then the outer problem attains a minimum.

Proof.
Compactness and continuity imply that the composition is continuous on a compact set, so the minimum is attained by the Weierstrass theorem. \square

G.3 Inner problem: necessary optimality conditions

For fixed \mathcal{L}, the inner problem is

\min_{s,\rho,\tau} J(s,\rho,\tau)

subject to equality constraints from dynamics and inequality safety constraints. Let \lambda_i denote Lagrange multipliers for equality constraints g_i=0, and \mu_j for inequality constraints h_j\le 0. The Karush-Kuhn-Tucker conditions are

\nabla_{s,\rho,\tau} J + \sum_i \lambda_i \nabla g_i + \sum_j \mu_j \nabla h_j = 0,

\mu_j\ge 0,\quad \mu_j h_j=0.

G.4 Practical relaxation

Full bilevel optimization is computationally intractable for realistic RNA/lipid nanoparticle systems. Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics therefore adopts a hierarchical relaxation:

· The outer loop selects from a library of N_{\mathcal{L}} validated lipid nanoparticle formulations, typically 10\le N_{\mathcal{L}}\le 50.
· The inner loop solves a robust model-predictive control problem over sequence, dose, and schedule, given delivery uncertainty bounds derived from the selected formulation’s empirical biodistribution profile.
· For N_{\mathcal{L}}\le 50 and horizon H\le 10, the outer problem is tractable via branch-and-bound or warm-started enumeration.
· The outer loop is updated only when new lipid nanoparticle platforms complete GMP validation, which occurs on timescales of months to years.

