Appendix H. Programmability Assessment and Illustrative PCSK9 Worked Example

H.1 Multi-criteria programmability vector

For a disease d, define

\mathbf{M}(d)=[m_{\text{obs}},m_{\text{mod}},m_{\text{ctrl}},m_{\text{deliv}},m_{\text{safety}}]\in[0,1]^5,

with the following operational scoring rules:

· m_{\text{obs}}: fraction of clinically relevant state dimensions that are directly or indirectly measurable with acceptable latency.
· m_{\text{mod}}: calibrated AUROC or expected calibration error on an unseen perturbation benchmark.
· m_{\text{ctrl}}: fraction of admissible dose range that moves the predicted state toward x^* without leaving \mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}}.
· m_{\text{deliv}}: specificity score, such as target-to-liver accumulation ratio in a relevant in vivo model.
· m_{\text{safety}}: therapeutic index, defined as \text{TD}_{50}/\text{ED}_{50}, mapped to [0,1] by a monotone transformation.

H.2 Aggregation

When a scalar summary is required, use the weighted geometric mean:

\mathcal{D}_T=
\left(\prod_{k=1}^5 m_k^{w_k}\right)^{1/\sum_k w_k},

where w_k>0 are domain-specific weights.

H.3 Programmability frontier

A disease is programmable at tier r\in\{1,2,3,4\} if the vector \mathbf{M}(d) exceeds pre-specified thresholds for that tier. The frontier is the set of diseases for which tier-4 closed-loop operation is supported by current sensing, modeling, actuation, and delivery technologies.

H.4 Illustrative PCSK9 model

Consider a simplified discrete-time model of hepatic PCSK9 regulation:

x_{t+1}=A x_t+B \rho_t+w_t,

with state

x_t=
\begin{bmatrix}
m_t\\
p_t\\
L_t
\end{bmatrix},

where m_t is hepatic PCSK9 mRNA, p_t is serum PCSK9 protein, and L_t is LDL-C. The observation is

y_t=
\begin{bmatrix}
p_t\\
L_t
\end{bmatrix}+v_t.

The intervention \rho_t is the siRNA dose. A nominal linearization might be

A=
\begin{bmatrix}
0.9 & 0 & 0\\
0.5 & 0.95 & 0\\
0 & 0.3 & 0.85
\end{bmatrix},
\quad
B=
\begin{bmatrix}
-0.05\\
-0.02\\
0
\end{bmatrix},

C=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & 1 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}.

The desired steady state is an LDL-C reduction of at least 50%. The safety constraint is ALT/AST \le 2\times baseline.

H.5 Kalman filter for PCSK9 state estimation

Using the linear Gaussian model with known covariances Q and R, the Kalman filter equations of Appendix C.2 provide \hat x_t and P_t. The state estimate is then used in the model-predictive control objective.

H.6 Model-predictive control formulation

The model-predictive control problem at time t minimizes

\sum_{k=0}^{H-1}\left(\|x_{t+k}-x^*\|_Q^2+\lambda \rho_{t+k}^2\right)+\|x_{t+H}-x^*\|_{Q_f}^2

subject to the linear dynamics, dose limits, and safety constraints

x_{\text{ALT},t+k}\le 2 x_{\text{ALT},\text{baseline}}.

Only the first dose \rho_t^* is applied.

H.7 Conformal safety filter

Using split conformal prediction on a calibration set of in vivo dose-response data, construct

C(x_t,\rho_t)=\{z:\|z-\hat f(x_t,\rho_t)\|\le s_{(q)}\}.

The safety filter then requires that for all \tilde{x}_{t+1}\in C(x_t,\rho_t),

\tilde{x}_{\text{ALT},t+1}\le 2 x_{\text{ALT},\text{baseline}}.

If the condition fails, the dose is reduced until it holds.

H.8 Online patient-specific learning

After each measurement, the patient-specific knockdown efficiency \theta_i is updated using the recursive ridge update of Appendix C.5. The updated posterior feeds into the next model-predictive control solve.

H.9 Limitations of the illustrative model

This linear model is illustrative and omits several known biological feedback loops, including SREBP-2-mediated LDL receptor upregulation and compensatory cholesterol synthesis. A realistic Adaptive Programmable Therapeutics model for PCSK9 control would require a nonlinear virtual hepatocyte model including the SREBP-2 pathway, patient-specific parameters for baseline LDL-C synthesis rates, and validation against longitudinal patient data. The linear example demonstrates the control architecture, not a validated clinical model.

